T = {
'w8-more-than-health':"Take five quiet minutes tonight. On paper, list the eight OneUP dimensions. Next to each, write one word for how that area feels today, such as 'steady', 'tired' or 'ignored'. Circle the area that feels least well. Then choose one small, easy action for it tomorrow, something that takes ten minutes or less, and write down when you will do it.",
'w8-wheel':"Draw a simple circle with eight spokes and label each with a OneUP dimension. Rate each area from one to five and mark the score on its spoke, then join the dots. Look at the shape. Pick the loosest spoke and write one small step you could take for it this week. Keep the drawing and redo it in a month to see what changed.",
'w8-learning':"Choose one topic you are genuinely curious about. Find one short source on it, such as a ten-minute video, a podcast episode or a few pages of a book. Schedule it into a gap you already have, like a commute or tea break, on three days this week. Afterwards, tell one person one thing you learned.",
'w8-kindness':"Today, do one small, specific act of kindness for someone you usually overlook, such as learning their name or thanking them properly. Tonight, also notice one moment when you spoke harshly to yourself, and quietly rewrite that sentence the way you would say it to a good friend. Note both in a line or two.",
'w8-surroundings':"Pick the space where you spend the most waking time. Stand in it for a minute and notice the light, noise, clutter and smell. Choose one change that costs nothing, such as opening a window, clearing one surface or moving a lamp. Keep it for a week and notice whether your mood or focus feels any different.",
'w8-clutter':"Set a timer for fifteen minutes. Choose one small area: a drawer, a bag or your phone's home screen. For each item, ask: do I use it, do I need it, would I get it again? Keep, pass on, recycle or delete accordingly. When the timer ends, stop, even if you are not finished, and notice how the space feels.",
'w8-financial-freedom':"For the next seven days, write down every payment you make, however small, in a notebook or notes app. Do not try to change anything yet. At the end of the week, group the payments into four or five rough categories and notice which surprised you. Write one sentence about how you feel. This is general learning, not financial advice.",
'ah-tiny-gains':"Pick one habit you have been meaning to build. Shrink it to a version so small it feels almost silly, such as one push-up, one page or one minute of tidying. Do that tiny version every day for two weeks, and put a mark on a calendar each time. Focus on the streak, not the size.",
'ah-systems':"Write down one goal you care about. Underneath, write the system that would move you towards it in one sentence: 'I will [action] at [time] in [place].' Make the action small enough for a busy day. Follow the system for one week, ticking each day you show up, and judge yourself only by the ticks.",
'ah-identity':"Complete this sentence: 'I am the kind of person who…' with an identity you want, such as 'moves every day' or 'keeps promises to myself'. Then list three small actions that would be votes for that identity. Do one today. Each evening this week, count how many votes you cast.",
'ah-four-laws':"Choose one habit you want. Write the four laws down: obvious, attractive, easy, satisfying. Next to each, write one small change you could make for this habit, such as placing a cue in sight or pairing it with music you like. Pick the one that seems weakest right now and make that change today.",
'ah-stacking':"List five things you already do every day without fail. Choose one as an anchor and write a stack: 'After I [current habit], I will [new habit].' Keep the new habit under two minutes. Stick the sentence somewhere you will see it, such as the fridge or your phone lock screen, and try it for seven days.",
'ah-environment':"Walk through one room and ask which habits it makes easy. Move one item to support a habit you want, such as a book on the pillow or a water bottle on the desk. Then write the two-minute version of that habit. For one week, do only the two-minute version and stop if you want to.",
'ah-invisible':"Identify one unwanted habit and the cue that usually starts it. Today, make that cue harder to see: move it, hide it, mute it or log out. Put a better option in its place that meets the same need. Notice over three days how often the old urge appears, and be gentle with yourself if it does.",
'ah-one-space':"Choose one space and give it one clear purpose, such as 'this chair is for reading' or 'this table is for study'. Add a small signal, like a lamp, cloth or plant, that you use only for that activity. For a week, do that activity there and avoid doing other things in that spot.",
'ah-commitment':"Think of one decision you keep remaking in tempting moments. In a calm moment today, make one choice that makes the better option the default, such as setting a plug timer, unsubscribing from sale emails, or planning a walk with a friend. Keep it gentle and adjust it if it feels too strict. This is not financial advice.",
'ah-contract':"Choose one habit and one supportive person. Write a short, friendly agreement: what you will do, how often, and a small light-hearted consequence if you skip, such as making the tea. Both sign it or confirm by message. Share a simple tracker and check in once a week. Keep any stakes tiny. This is not financial advice.",
'miy-sabotage':"Think of one goal you keep getting in the way of. Write down the behaviour that blocks it. Then ask: 'If this behaviour were protecting me from something, what would it be?' Write whatever comes up without judging it. Finally, write one gentler way to meet that same need. If this stirs heavy feelings, pause and consider support.",
'miy-triggers':"The next time you notice a reaction that feels bigger than the moment, pause for three slow breaths and silently name the feeling. Later, when calm, write three lines: what happened, what it reminded you of, and what you needed. Do this for a few triggers over two weeks and look for patterns. Seek support if it feels overwhelming.",
'miy-eq':"Set three gentle reminders on your phone for tomorrow. Each time one goes off, pause and ask: 'What am I feeling right now, and where do I notice it in my body?' Try to name the feeling with a precise word rather than 'fine' or 'bad'. In the evening, look at the three words and notice what they tell you.",
'miy-future':"Write a short paragraph describing an ordinary day in the life of the person you hope to become in a year. Underline one habit in that paragraph. Tomorrow, do a tiny version of that habit, even if you do not feel ready. Each evening this week, ask, 'What would my future self do with thirty spare minutes?'",
'btb-tendencies':"Think about two promises: one you made to someone else and one you made to yourself. Which did you keep more easily? Use that clue to guess which tendency might fit you. Then redesign one stalled habit to suit it, for example by adding a reason, a partner or a sense of choice. Try it for one week.",
'btb-foundation':"Pick one foundation: sleep, movement, food and drink, or order. Choose one small step for it, such as phone out of the bedroom by a set time, a ten-minute walk, a glass of water with each meal, or a two-minute evening tidy. Do it for seven days and notice whether any other part of life feels easier.",
'btb-monitoring':"Choose one behaviour you suspect you underestimate, such as screen time, snacks or small spends. Track it for seven days using the simplest method available, such as a note on your phone or a built-in report. Do not try to change it yet. At the end of the week, write one sentence about what surprised you. This is not financial advice.",
'btb-abstainer':"Pick one tempting thing you would like to have less of. For one week, try the Abstainer approach: none at all. For the following week, try the Moderator approach: a small, planned amount. At the end, compare how each felt. Which brought calm, and which brought bargaining? Use that to choose your approach.",
'btb-loopholes':"For the next few days, keep a short list of the excuses you hear yourself making to skip a habit. Next to each, write which type of loophole it might be, such as tomorrow logic or 'this doesn't count'. Then choose one upcoming occasion, like a festival or trip, and decide calmly in advance how you will handle it.",
'btb-convenience':"Choose one good habit and one unwanted habit. For the good one, remove a single barrier today, such as preparing things the night before. For the unwanted one, add a single small barrier, such as moving it out of sight or logging out. Keep both changes for a week and notice what happens.",
'btb-clarity':"Pick one vague intention, such as 'be healthier' or 'save more'. Ask 'why?' three times to reach the value underneath, and write it down. Then rewrite the intention as one specific action with a time and place. If two values are pulling against each other, decide how you will balance them this week. This is not financial advice.",
'btb-scheduling':"Choose one habit that keeps slipping. Look at your week and pick a realistic slot that matches your energy. Put it in your calendar as a recurring event with a reminder. Add one enjoyable activity as a protected slot too. If you miss a slot, simply return at the next one. This is not financial advice.",
'bks-mind-body':"This is optional and only if it feels comfortable. During an ordinary, calm moment today, pause and gently notice your shoulders, jaw and breathing for about thirty seconds, without trying to change anything. If this brings up anything distressing, stop and do something grounding instead, such as looking around the room. Consider reaching out to a qualified professional if difficult feelings arise.",
'bks-connection':"Think of one person with whom you feel at ease. This week, arrange a small, low-pressure moment together, such as a short walk, a shared meal or a phone call. Notice how you feel during and after. If connecting feels hard right now, that is understandable; start with something very small, like a friendly message.",
'bks-many-paths':"Make a short list of everyday activities that generally help you feel calm and settled, such as walking, music, prayer, cooking or time with a pet. Keep the list somewhere handy. Separately, note one place where you could find a qualified professional if you or someone you know ever needed support.",
'bks-self-compassion':"Notice one harsh phrase you say to yourself this week. Write it down. Then write how a kind, wise friend might say it instead. Read the kinder version aloud once a day for a few days. If you notice persistent or intense distress, please consider speaking with a qualified professional.",
'ms-two-mindsets':"Write down three areas where you tend to say 'I'm not good at this'. For each, rewrite the sentence by adding 'yet' and one small next step you could take to improve. Choose one and take that step this week. Notice whether the 'yet' changes how the challenge feels.",
'ms-failure':"Think of a recent setback, big or small. Answer three questions in writing: What happened? What can I learn? What will I try differently next time? Keep each answer to a sentence or two. Then share the 'try differently' answer with one trusted person, or simply put it into action at the next chance.",
'ms-effort':"Choose one skill you have avoided because it needs effort. Practise it for ten minutes on three days this week. After each session, write one thing that improved, however small, and one strategy you might change next time. Rest between sessions and notice how your view of the effort shifts.",
'ms-praise':"This week, catch three moments to praise someone's process instead of their traits. Be specific: name the effort, strategy or persistence you noticed. Also catch one moment where you would normally call yourself 'smart' or 'useless', and replace it with a sentence about what you did or will do differently.",
'ms-relationships':"Think of a recent disagreement with someone close. Write down the label you put on them or yourself, such as 'selfish' or 'difficult'. Then rewrite it as a specific behaviour in a specific situation. Next time you speak, try asking, 'What can we learn from this, and how could we handle it better?'",
'p48-awareness':"Over the next week, notice moments when someone tries to influence you through flattery, guilt, urgency or secrecy. Simply name the tactic silently. Before agreeing to anything, take a moment to check the decision against your own values. Write down one situation and how you chose to respond. If you face serious pressure or harassment, seek help.",
'p48-say-less':"In your next meeting or important conversation, make a point of pausing for a breath before you respond. Aim to make one or two clear points instead of many. If someone falls silent after a request, notice the urge to fill the gap, and instead calmly ask a question or take time to think.",
'p48-company':"Over the next week, after spending time with different people, jot down one word for how you feel: energised, calm, drained or anxious. Look for patterns. If one relationship regularly leaves you depleted, think of one kind, clear boundary you could set, and practise saying it aloud before you use it.",
'p48-plan':"Choose one decision you are currently facing. Write three questions: What could go wrong? What will this look like in a year? Who else does this affect? Answer each briefly. Then set one first step and one date to check in and adjust. Keep the planning to a single page so it leads to action.",
'pon-present':"Choose one routine activity tomorrow, such as drinking tea, brushing your teeth or walking to the bus. For the first minute of it, put your phone away and notice what you can see, hear, smell and feel. When your mind wanders, gently bring it back. Do this once a day for a week.",
'pon-watch-mind':"Sit quietly for two minutes with a timer. Each time a thought appears, label it with a single word, such as 'planning', 'worrying' or 'remembering', and let it pass without following it. When the timer ends, notice how you feel. Try this once a day for a week. Stop if it feels uncomfortable.",
'pon-acceptance':"Think of one small situation you are resisting, such as a delay or an inconvenience. Say to yourself, 'This is what is happening right now.' Take three slow breaths. Then ask, 'What, if anything, can I do next?' Take that one step, or let it be if there is nothing to do.",
'pon-inner-body':"Sit or lie comfortably for one minute. Bring attention to your hands and notice any sensation, such as warmth, tingling or heaviness. Then move attention to your feet. Keep your eyes open if that feels better. If this feels uncomfortable in any way, stop and focus on an outside anchor, like the feel of the chair.",
'aop-win-win':"Think of a current disagreement or request. Write down what you want, then what you think the other person wants and why. Ask them one open question to check. Then sketch one option that would give each of you something you value, and propose it politely.",
'aop-own-emotions':"Choose a short phrase to remind yourself of your goal in hard conversations, such as 'solve, not win'. The next time you feel a strong reaction, take one slow breath, say the phrase silently, and only then reply. Afterwards, do something to release the tension, such as a walk or a chat with a friend.",
'aop-difficult':"Before your next interaction with a difficult person, prepare one sentence that acknowledges their feelings and one that offers them a graceful way out, such as 'I can see why it looked that way.' Use them calmly. If the behaviour becomes abusive, set a clear limit and seek help.",
'aop-relationships':"This week, offer three small courtesies without expecting anything back: remember and use someone's name, follow up on something they mentioned, or share something useful. Also keep one small promise exactly as you said you would. Notice how these moments feel and how people respond.",
}
